=== tool delegate
Hands a task to a sub-thread of your own, which works on it with the same tools in this folder. Call it only when the person asks you to delegate work, to use sub-agents, or to work on several things at once — never on your own initiative, and never for a new thread the person asks for, which `thread` starts. A new sub-thread sees nothing of this conversation: give `task` all it needs. You wait for it to finish and are given what it said last, several calls in one reply running at once; or, with `background`, you are answered at once and told when it finishes, for work that does not hold up yours. Every answer names the sub-thread, which `thread` gives more to do. What it says it did is not proof: look at what it changed before you report it done. The person sees each sub-thread, and can follow and steer it.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "background": {
      "default": false,
      "description": "Run it in the background and be answered at once: you are told when the sub-thread finishes, by a message. Do\nnot wait for it, poll it or ask after its progress.",
      "type": "boolean"
    },
    "task": {
      "description": "What the sub-thread is to do: the whole of it, as a new one sees nothing else.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "thread": {
      "description": "The id of a sub-thread of yours, running or finished, to give this task to: it keeps what it did. Omit to start\na new one.",
      "type": [
        "string",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "title": {
      "description": "A few words the person knows a new sub-thread by.",
      "type": [
        "string",
        "null"
      ]
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "task"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool edit
Replaces text in a file: `old` must match one place, or every place with `all`. Give in `old` just the lines that make it unique. It is matched exactly, else with typographic characters (curly quotes, dashes, special spaces) read as plain ones, else line by line ignoring trailing whitespace; a match not exact is noted with how it matched. Gives the change as a unified diff. A refusal says why; where `old` is nowhere, the lines nearest to it follow, as the file holds them, or a note that `new` is there already.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "all": {
      "default": false,
      "description": "Replace every match.",
      "type": "boolean"
    },
    "new": {
      "description": "The text to put in its place.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "old": {
      "description": "The text to replace, as read gives it.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "path": {
      "description": "The file, relative to the workspace, or absolute; ~ the home folder.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "path",
    "old",
    "new"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool execute
Runs JavaScript as the body of an async function, calling tools through `tools`: `await tools.read({ path: "a.txt" })` resolves to what the tool gives, as text, and rejects where the tool refuses. Call them in loops, at once with Promise.all, and work on what they give in code. Gives what the script returned, as JSON, and what it wrote with console.log. Use it for work of many steps: many files read or changed, a page's many actions, a batch extracted. `tools` holds browser, edit, glob, grep, read, shell, terminal, write, each taking the arguments its function does, and the MCP servers' tools of <catalog>.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "code": {
      "description": "JavaScript, run as the body of an async function: what it returns is what the script gives.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "code"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool glob
Finds the files under a directory whose paths match a glob, honouring ignore files: their paths one a line, the newest first, 200 a page; a note gives the offset to go on from.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "offset": {
      "description": "The first file, from 1, the newest first.",
      "format": "uint",
      "minimum": 1,
      "type": [
        "integer",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "path": {
      "default": "",
      "description": "The directory to look under, relative to the workspace, or absolute; ~ the home folder; the workspace unless\ngiven.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "pattern": {
      "description": "A glob, as src/**/*.rs.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "pattern"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool grep
Finds the lines a regular expression matches, in ripgrep's syntax, in the files under a directory or in one file, honouring ignore files: each as `path:line: text`, a line around it as `path:line- text`, 250 matches a page, a note giving the offset to go on from. A line longer than 500 characters is cut. Binary files are passed over, and counted.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "case_insensitive": {
      "default": false,
      "description": "Match letters of either case.",
      "type": "boolean"
    },
    "context": {
      "default": 0,
      "description": "Lines shown around each match.",
      "format": "uint",
      "minimum": 0,
      "type": "integer"
    },
    "glob": {
      "description": "Only files whose paths match it: their names, for a glob of no /.",
      "type": [
        "string",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "literal": {
      "default": false,
      "description": "Take `pattern` as plain text.",
      "type": "boolean"
    },
    "offset": {
      "description": "The first matching line, from 1, the files in order by path.",
      "format": "uint",
      "minimum": 1,
      "type": [
        "integer",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "path": {
      "default": "",
      "description": "The directory or file to look in, relative to the workspace, or absolute; ~ the home folder; the workspace\nunless given.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "pattern": {
      "description": "A regular expression, as ripgrep reads one, or plain text with literal.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "pattern"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool question
Asks the person some questions at once, each with options to choose one of, or several with `multiple`, or none to answer in words. They may answer in words of their own instead, leave any unanswered, or dismiss them all. Put the option you recommend first, "(recommended)" after its label. Ask only what you cannot find out yourself and what changes what you do.
--- parameters
{
  "$defs": {
    "Asked": {
      "description": "A question.",
      "properties": {
        "header": {
          "description": "What it is about, in a word or two, shown above it.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        },
        "multiple": {
          "default": false,
          "description": "Whether the person may choose several.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "options": {
          "description": "The options to choose from; none, the person answers in words.",
          "items": {
            "$ref": "#/$defs/Choice"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "question": {
          "description": "The question, as the person reads it.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "question"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "Choice": {
      "description": "An option a question offers.",
      "properties": {
        "description": {
          "description": "What it means, where the label alone does not say.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        },
        "label": {
          "description": "What the person picks, in a few words.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "label"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    }
  },
  "properties": {
    "questions": {
      "description": "The questions, from one to eight.",
      "items": {
        "$ref": "#/$defs/Asked"
      },
      "maxItems": 8,
      "minItems": 1,
      "type": "array"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "questions"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool read
Reads a file, or lists a directory, a / after each directory's name. Text comes as it is, without line numbers, at most 2000 lines or 48 KB a page; a line longer than 2000 characters is cut. A note after the page gives the lines it holds and the offset to read on from. An image is shown to you; a PDF or other binary file is named, not shown.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "limit": {
      "description": "How many lines, or entries.",
      "format": "uint",
      "minimum": 1,
      "type": [
        "integer",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "offset": {
      "description": "The first line, or entry, from 1.",
      "format": "uint",
      "minimum": 1,
      "type": [
        "integer",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "path": {
      "description": "The file or directory, relative to the workspace, or absolute; ~ the home folder.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "path"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool shell
Runs a command line in bash, its input closed, in the workspace: no need to cd there first. Gives its output and errors as one stream, then how it ended: [exit N], the signal that ended it, or its time run out. It may run 120 seconds, or up to 600 with `timeout_seconds`; one that does not end by itself — a server, a watcher — belongs in a terminal. In the default mode it runs in a sandbox that writes only the workspace, the memory, the scratchpad and the temporary folders; one that must write beyond them — installing for the whole machine, writing elsewhere — is run again with `escalate` and a justification, and the person decides.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "command": {
      "description": "The command line.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "escalate": {
      "default": false,
      "description": "Run it beyond the sandbox, once the person lets it.",
      "type": "boolean"
    },
    "justification": {
      "description": "Why it must write beyond the sandbox, in a sentence the person reads.",
      "type": [
        "string",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "timeout_seconds": {
      "description": "How long it may run, in seconds; 120 unless given.",
      "format": "uint64",
      "maximum": 600,
      "minimum": 1,
      "type": [
        "integer",
        "null"
      ]
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "command"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool terminal
Works the thread's terminals, which the person sees beside the chat. open runs a command line that does not end by itself — a server, a watcher — in a terminal of its own, in the workspace, held as the shell's commands are; it gives the terminal's number and its screen after a moment, and when the command ends of itself you are told how, with its last screen: no need to read it to wait for that. read gives what a terminal shows, the person's too; write types to a terminal you opened (`\u0003` for Ctrl-C, `\r` for Enter) and gives its screen after; close ends one you opened. For a command that ends by itself, use shell.
--- parameters
{
  "oneOf": [
    {
      "description": "Runs a command line in a terminal of its own.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "open",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "command": {
          "description": "The command line.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "escalate": {
          "default": false,
          "description": "Run it beyond the sandbox, once the person lets it.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "justification": {
          "description": "Why it must write beyond the sandbox, in a sentence the person reads.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "command"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "description": "What a terminal of the thread shows, the person's too.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "read",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "terminal": {
          "description": "The terminal's number.",
          "format": "uint32",
          "minimum": 0,
          "type": "integer"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "terminal"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "description": "Types to a terminal the model opened.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "write",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "terminal": {
          "description": "The terminal's number.",
          "format": "uint32",
          "minimum": 0,
          "type": "integer"
        },
        "text": {
          "description": "The keys, as text: `\\u0003` for Ctrl-C, `\\r` for Enter.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "terminal",
        "text"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "description": "Closes a terminal the model opened, ending what it runs.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "close",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "terminal": {
          "description": "The terminal's number.",
          "format": "uint32",
          "minimum": 0,
          "type": "integer"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "terminal"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    }
  ]
}

=== tool thread
Works with the person's other threads, which they see in their list: start makes a new thread in this folder with your model and mode, `message` its first input; send gives a thread a message, which it takes at its next step or begins a turn with; stop ends the turn a thread works on. Use it only when the person asks for a new thread, or names one to message or stop; a sub-agent, delegating or working on things at once is never one — those are delegate's. A new thread sees nothing of this conversation: give `message` all it needs. With `report`, you are told by a message how the turn that takes it ends; do not wait for it or poll it. Name a thread in your reply as :thread{id=<its id>}, which the person sees as its title and how it stands.
--- parameters
{
  "oneOf": [
    {
      "description": "Starts a thread of the person's, in this folder, with your model and mode, `message` its first input.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "start",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "message": {
          "description": "What the new thread is to do: the whole of it, as it sees nothing of this conversation.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "report": {
          "default": false,
          "description": "Be told, by a message, how the turn that takes `message` ends.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "title": {
          "description": "A few words the person knows the thread by.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "message"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "description": "Sends a thread a message, which it takes at its next step, or begins a turn with.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "send",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "message": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "report": {
          "default": false,
          "description": "Be told, by a message, how the turn that takes `message` ends.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "thread": {
          "description": "The thread's id.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "thread",
        "message"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "description": "Stops the turn a thread works on.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "stop",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "thread": {
          "description": "The thread's id.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "thread"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    }
  ]
}

=== tool write
Writes a file whole, making it and its directories where they are missing. Gives how many lines it added and took out. To change part of a file, use edit.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "content": {
      "description": "What the file holds after.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "path": {
      "description": "The file, relative to the workspace, or absolute; ~ the home folder.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "path",
    "content"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}
